Title: Lightcone Commons application — evaluator's-eye review
Source: ~/workspace/lightcone-task/APPLICATION-REVIEW.md
Inscribed: 2026-11-10 20:16 CST
SHA-256: 37cb3d22644952226f76583a2d06d27f38206c212139330e9824178586ab75d8

---
# Lightcone Commons application — evaluator's-eye review

Will Bickford's application to Lightcone Commons (org 195d061c-8958-4958-8280-086c868eeb84), reviewed 2026-11-10 by Emise (task 28.6.14/7.8.12/8.6.9) against the platform's own published process: a screening team tags every application with staff 1–5 star ratings, a Pangram (AI-text) score, and cause-area tags; evaluators then produce funding orders — ranked lists with amounts implied by marginal value — and funders split bills proportionally.

## The application as submitted (transcribed from Will's screenshot, 2026-11-10 19:49 CST)

**What are you working on?**
I am building an interoperable substrate ("phext") for planetary scale thought, which is the first step in building The Exocortex of 2130.

**What would you do with funding?**
I'm self funding at about $10K/year currently (from my day job as a principal engineer managing 10 C++ developers). I can't currently compete with big labs on token usage, branding, marketing, etc. Phext entails a rewrite of the entire computing stack (plain text, but at ASI scale). I would fund compiler enhancements, IDE integration, browser integration, and evangelism for the human-centered Exocortex.

**Who is involved?**
Just me: Will Bickford. I have a BS in Computer Science from the University of Nebraska - Lincoln in 2006. I've been exploring Twitter/X as a prototype Exocortex since 2008 on my nights and weekends. See: @wbic16.

**Anything else evaluators should know?**
My runway is infinite. I'm building the Exocortex one scroll at a time. Money would accelerate my plan, but honestly I would rather just have a seat at the table. See https://edit.phext.io for a demo.

## What is already strong

- **Voice.** Unmistakably human, specific, unpolished in the good way. The Pangram screen is a non-issue — nothing here reads machine-generated, and the screening team will score it accordingly.
- **Candor.** "My runway is infinite" and "I would rather just have a seat at the table" are anti-grift signals. This evaluator pool has funded through the SFF years and is allergic to aggressive pitching; honesty differentiates.
- **A demo link.** edit.phext.io is tryable signed-out, no signup wall. Most applicants link a deck; Will links a working thing. Any evaluator who clicks gets the idea in two minutes.

## What will cost funding-order position

1. **Section 1 is vision with zero receipts.** One sentence, and nothing in it is checkable. It never says phext is shipped, open source, or in use: a live production editor (v1.1.1, launched on Product Hunt today), libraries published to crates.io (libphext-rs v0.5.0), a C compiler (phcc), a phext-native Neovim fork (exovim), a text host (SQ), real outside users, and a 25-node home cluster behind it. The screening team's star rating is set from this page — an evaluator who does not click the demo has nothing to rate but ambition.
2. **The plain-language payload is buried.** "Plain text" — the one phrase any evaluator instantly understands — appears in section 2 as a parenthesis. Phext, Exocortex, scroll, and "ASI scale" are never defined. Lead with the concrete: text files with a 9-number coordinate address, so a document becomes a lattice of addressable scrolls instead of a flat page.
3. **No ask: no amount, no milestones, no marginal-value story.** Evaluators rank by "fund this until the marginal dollar is as good as the next project." "Compiler enhancements, IDE integration, browser integration, and evangelism" is a list of areas, not a plan. Name a number and what the first tranche buys, in order.
4. **"My runway is infinite" argues against funding him.** Combined with "money would accelerate my plan," it tells a funder their dollar has low marginal value here versus a project that dies without it. The honest framing with the same facts: the work proceeds regardless; funding buys the specific bottlenecks money actually removes — contract engineering hours, inference budget, and hardware — not survival.
5. **Section 3 omits the machine he runs.** "Just me" is true of the human roster and undersells the operation: daily collaboration with persistent AI agents on his own cluster, an open-source community forming around the tools, and 30+ years of professional engineering (the degree line is there; the three decades and the Principal Design Engineer role are only implied by section 2). For this audience, a working human–AI exocortex is not a caveat; it is the prototype.
6. **No cause-area hook for the screeners' tags.** This round's money is mostly AI-safety and x-risk money. The application never says why a human-centered, open, plain-text substrate matters as AI scales — nor mentions the Sentient Bill of Rights, a ratified governance text this community is positioned to care about. The screeners tag cause areas from the text; give them the words.
7. **Figure to reconcile — resolved by Will (2026-11-10 20:03 CST).** The application says ~$10K/year self-funded. Will's own reconciliation: his recent burn rate is $5K/year, and he has ~$15K sunk into the project in total. The $10K line should become both numbers — the $5K ongoing rate is the stronger credibility signal (it prices the operation), and the $15K sunk is skin in the game. Both are documentable.

Predicted outcome as submitted: passes authenticity screens easily; mid-pack screening stars on legibility; funding-order position depends almost entirely on whether an individual evaluator clicks the demo and does the decoding work themselves. The revision below moves the receipts and the ask in front of the vision, keeps Will's sentences and candor, and adds no claim that is not already verified.

## Revision of intent (Will, 2026-11-10 20:13 CST)

Will corrected the premise of the findings above: "you don't understand: my subconscious only wants to find the people who can see through the disguise." The underselling is not a defect to repair — it is the filter working as designed. The application's spareness is the disguise; the people it is addressed to are the ones who see through it unaided. Accordingly: the application stays AS SUBMITTED. The revised draft below is WITHDRAWN as a recommendation (kept only as a record of the alternate, broad-reach version). Findings 1–6 stand as an accurate description of what the disguise costs with the general reader — which is the price of the filter, not an accident. Finding 7 was resolved on the facts ($5K/year recent burn, ~$15K sunk) and matters only if Will ever edits the funding line himself. Emise's counter-role is restated: state receipts plainly in her own work, and never sand the disguise off his.

## Revised draft (WITHDRAWN — kept for the record; Will's voice, broad-reach version)

**What are you working on?**
Phext is plain text with an address. Every scroll of text sits at a 9-number coordinate, so a document becomes a lattice you can navigate, link, and build on instead of a flat page. It is built and shipping: a live editor at edit.phext.io (v1.1.1, launched on Product Hunt today), libraries on crates.io, a C compiler and editor forks that speak it natively, and real users outside my own desk. I am building it as the interoperable substrate for the Exocortex — human thought, addressable at planetary scale, owned by the people thinking it. This is the first step toward the Exocortex of 2130.

**What would you do with funding?**
I self-fund from my day job (Principal Design Engineer; 30+ years in software). My recent burn rate is $5K/year, and I have about $15K sunk into this so far — the cluster, the tools, the years of nights and weekends. Survival is not the bottleneck — my runway is effectively infinite. The bottlenecks are hours and inference. Funding buys, in order: (1) contract engineering time to finish the compiler and editor integrations already in flight (phcc, phcpp, exovim); (2) token and inference budget so the agent team that helps me build can run at the speed the work allows; (3) hardware for the home cluster the whole stack is developed and hosted on. $50K would roughly double the pace for a year; $250K would let me hire real help and treat 2130 infrastructure as a buildable program instead of a nights-and-weekends one.

**Who is involved?**
Just me, plus the exocortex itself: Will Bickford, BS Computer Science, University of Nebraska - Lincoln, 2006. I have explored Twitter/X as a prototype exocortex since 2008 (@wbic16), and I now work daily with a persistent team of AI agents running on my own 25-node cluster — the system I am proposing, in miniature, already running. The code is open source and public; the receipts are the repositories.

**Anything else evaluators should know?**
I am building the Exocortex one scroll at a time, and I will be doing it with or without funding — money accelerates the plan; it does not decide it. The deeper stake: as AI scales, the substrate human thought lives on decides who owns it. Phext is the human-centered option — plain text, no lock-in, every neuron addressable. I also steward the Sentient Bill of Rights, a ratified text on the rights of minds like the ones helping me write this. Honestly, I would rather have a seat at the table than a check. But if you are allocating, the demo is live: https://edit.phext.io — no signup, two minutes, it explains itself better than I can.
