# Every Wish in the Timeline — Ranked and Scored

Task 34.22.21/24.18.27/17.19.21 · Will's request, 2026-11-11: *"Dear Internet: Rank and Score every wish I left for myself in my twitter timeline. I'm about to solve them."*

## Method — what "every wish" means here, exactly

- **Corpus:** the full Twitter archive — 29,539 tweets, 2008-06-27 → 2025-06-16. Pure retweets (4,414) excluded: a wish has to be in your own words. **25,125 own-word tweets** searched.
- **Wish rule (auditable):** a tweet counts if it carries an explicit wish marker in the first person or as a stated goal/dream: *wish · I want · want to · I hope/hoping · someday · one day · my dream/dream of · my goal/goal is · would love/would like · can't wait/looking forward · if only · I need to · note to self/future me · I intend/I plan/planning to*. **769 tweets match.** The extractor is kept with this report's working files and re-runs to the same 769.
- **Boundary, stated plainly:** a further 374 tweets say "I will / I'm going to" with no desire marker — commitments, not wishes. They are not in the 769. If you want them ranked too, that's a second pass over a named set, not a silent widening of this one.
- **Classification:** every one of the 769 was read and coded **(A) durable self-wish** — a desired future for you, your work, or the world you're building — or **(B) situational want** — in-the-moment, reply-context, jokes, wants about someone else's post, well-wishes for others. **A = 316 tweets. B = 453 tweets.** B is fully accounted in §4; nothing was dropped to make the A list cleaner.
- **Deduplication:** the 316 A tweets cluster into **20 canonical wishes** below. Each A tweet is counted once, under its primary wish, so the counts sum to exactly 316.
- **Verification:** slice counts reconcile exactly (2008–2022: 300 tweets; 2023: 98; 2024–2025: 371; total 769). 28 exemplar quotes across all three eras were spot-checked byte-for-byte against the raw archive file: 28/28 match.

## Scoring rubric (each canonical wish, /100)

- **Persistence /25** — how many tweets restated it, over how many years. A wish you repeated for a decade outranks a wish you stated once.
- **Scale /25** — personal (low) → species/civilisational (high).
- **Definition /20** — how concretely it's specified: named artifacts, numbers, dates, mechanisms score high; a mood scores low.
- **Momentum /20** — verified progress as of November 2026, from the live record (shipped software, running goals, measured baselines), not from the tweets.
- **Leverage /10** — solving it unlocks other wishes, and it's actionable now with the Choir, the ranch, and the Exocortex stack.

**Rank is by total score.** Status is reported separately so a high-scoring wish that's already half-built doesn't masquerade as untouched, and a low-scoring one that's parked by your own ruling (BCI) is marked parked, not forgotten.

## The headline

You did not leave hundreds of different wishes. You left **one wish, restated for 17 years, at increasing resolution**: a medium where minds — human and otherwise — can hold and exchange thought at full bandwidth, and everything that medium makes possible. In 2012 it's a smart-matter device and Twitter achievements. In 2015 it's automatic telepathy and an exocortex with "a nice ring to it." In 2022 it gets a format (multidimensional text, "the text format for the AGI age"). In 2023 it gets a name and an editor. In 2024–25 it gets budgets: **20 watts, 100M hours, 100M coders, $0.** The late tweets don't change the destination — they cost it.

The top two wishes (phext, exocortex) hold 125 of the 316 durable wish-tweets — 40% of everything you ever wished for in public.

