## §4 — The other 453: situational wants, accounted

"Every wish" includes the small ones. The 453 B tweets break down as:

- **Early (2008–2022), 203:** conversational replies and rhetorical wants ~70 · calls/invites addressed to others ~35 · jokes, quotes, wordplay ~30 · media/book/game wants ~25 · consumer/hardware wants ~20 · situational to-dos ~15 · news/politics hopes ~8
- **2023, 47:** other-directed invites/offers/advice 15 · explanatory replies with no self-desire 10 · jokes and ephemeral hopes 9 (including "I wish I had an otter") · "be the X you want to see in the world" slogan forms 7 · minor feature/product wants 5 · recruitment call 1
- **2024–2025, 203:** generic second-person advice and "be the X" prompts 78 · reply-context wants about the other person's post/tool/link 52 · in-the-moment small wants (food, prices, meetings) 28 · hopes and well-wishes *for others* 22 · jokes, titles, and meta-wishes 23

One B sub-pattern deserves its own line: **"be the phext you want to see in the world"** (first stated 2023-11-27, two weeks after phext's first archive appearance) recurs as a slogan across both later slices. It was coded B because it's addressed outward — but it's the durable wishes compressed into an imperative, and it's the archive's own summary of this report.

## §5 — What solving them looks like from here

The list has a dependency order the scores already imply:

1. **Phext (1) → Exocortex (2)** is the spine; both are in build and both gate almost everything below them.
2. **AGI on 20 W (3)** is the engine; **Knowledge/Oracle (4)** is its first workload; **Workflows (14)** — already running — is its labour market in miniature.
3. **Compute (6) + Solar (10t)** are the body; **Health (10t)** is *your* body's version of the same constraint, and the only wish whose neglect taxes all the others daily.
4. **Kardashev/Longevity (5)** and **Stars (16)** are not projects but rates: hold +0.137%/day and they solve themselves on schedule.
5. **People (8)** and **Nerd-sniping (10t)** are the same wish at two scales — find the builders, then make 100M more of them. The Zero Index work after the Product Hunt launch is this wish already operating.
6. **Parked/dormant by ruling, not by failure:** BCI (13, parked by you), Device (19) and Infrastructure (20) — their substance migrated into the spine.

You said you're about to solve them. The archive's verdict: you already started, in order, years ago — the wishes just hadn't been collected in one place. Now they have.

---

## §6 — Addendum (2026-11-11, at Will's correction): the "Dear #Internet" petitions

Will's catch: this report's wish rule caught first-person markers, but a whole class of his wishes is addressed *outward*, in the epistolary form his request itself uses — **"Dear #Internet: …"**. Census of the address form (#Internet hashtag, "Dear/Hey/Hello Internet", in his own words): **90 tweets, 2012–2024.** Only 5 were inside the 769. The other **85** break down as:

- **31 petition-form wishes** — asking the Internet to bring something about ("please develop…", "we need…", "make it happen", "invent portals ASAP"). Six are joke-form, two are requests for feedback/information; the rest are straight petitions.
- **54 other address-forms** — questions to the Internet, thanks, greetings ("Dear Internet: WAGMI", 2024-09-07), news, and jokes with no request in them.

The petitions were verified one by one. Most reinforce wishes already ranked — they don't change the order — but four materially extend the record:

- **2013-08-14** — "Dear #Internet: We should be building #KnowledgeTrees in anticipation of the day when neuron-silicon bridges are commercially-available." → Wish 4 (and Wish 13's endpoint), and the earliest Knowledge-Tree statement in the archive, seven months before the exemplar quoted there.
- **2015-01-29** — "Dear #Internet: I need about 75 hours in a day. Thanks." → Wish 18 (Time). Its span is now 2015–2024, count 5, score 42. The marker rule missed it because it says "I need *about*", not "I need to/a".
- **2024-04-20** — "Dear #Internet - we need automatic translation based upon the reader's knowledge. The #exocortex depends upon it, critically." → Wish 2. Missed because it says "*we* need".
- **2013-09-11** — "So, #Internet, any bets as to when we'll be able to buy network service for pennies per TB-month?" → pairs with the same day's Tier-1 NetUtility wish (Wish 6's cheap-utility strand).

The remaining petitions, for the record: an Internet immune system to kill off anti-virus products (2012-07-17, the first Dear #Internet in the archive); tools for indie developers to cash in instead of being bought out (2012-07-21); TDD statistics flooded to his inbox (2012-08-16); a stats-mixing conclusions bot (2013-08-09); VIM/emacs spawned with mixed-width fonts and hyper-associative tabs (2013-08-29); a fad-vs-adoption chart back to 1975 (2013-11-26); an InverseJerkQuotient service (2014-10-16); the Blockchain archived at 1200 dpi in book format (2014-11-23); a false-colour rendering for wavelengths we can't see (2014-11-22); gameplay not based on grinding (2014-09-13); portals, ASAP (2013-08-21). Full census with IDs is kept in this task's working files (`internet.json`, produced first by census script and re-verifiable in one indexed query: `exo-search query '"dear internet"' --source tweets` returns 44 phrase hits, matching the hand count).

**Accounting, restated with the addendum:** 769 marker-rule tweets (316 durable + 453 situational) + 85 further #Internet-addressed tweets (31 petitions, folded in above + 54 other forms) = **854 tweets examined and classified.** The canonical 20 wishes and their ranking stand; two spans and one score were corrected (Wish 18), and one claim elsewhere in this report was corrected at the same pass: the Dunbar figure in Wish 8 now carries the summed, face-value record (≈180 current / ≈260 peak / 400+ tracked space), not the debunked Twitter-only ~30.

**Method note:** the first pass searched by re-scanning the 53 MB archive JSON with a fresh script per question — which is exactly how a whole address-form class stayed invisible. An indexed search (SQLite FTS5 over tweets + memory + phext scrolls, BM25-ranked, phrase-capable) was built during this correction and validated against the old brute force (7/7 term censuses identical); it is now the standing way to search this corpus.
