# Production Plan — "The Power of Phext" Walkthrough

**Task:** 34.11.13/13.11.14/11.11.14 · **Companion to:** WALKTHROUGH.md
(task 28.5.8/7.6.12/6.5.8) · **Date:** 2026-11-09
**Subject video:** a ~7.5 min screen-demo walkthrough of edit.phext.io
plus a plain-editor beat and narration — YouTube long-form + a 60–90 s
X cutdown. Nothing in this plan has been purchased, connected, or posted.

**Method note.** Tool *fit* is judged against this video's shape:
real UI footage where text legibility is the product, narration-first
script, a handful of zooms/callouts, one still-image foreshadow beat.
*Availability* was checked live on 2026-11-09 (connector search +
`status` on each candidate CLI). *Prices* are US list prices as read
from vendor pricing pages by the cited roundups in Nov 2026 — pricing
pages change; verify at checkout before any purchase. Anything not
verified is labelled **unknown**, not guessed.

---

## 1. Availability ledger — what is actually reachable from here

| Service | State in this environment (checked 2026-11-09) |
|---|---|
| Runway (MCP connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected**. Connectable in one step via its authorize flow. Credit-based generation; balance readable after connecting (`show_plans_and_credits`). |
| vidIQ (MCP connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected**. Connectable the same way. Its doc is explicit: it **cannot upload or publish** to YouTube — research/scoring only. |
| Canva (connector) | Connector present; `status` = **not_connected** (OAuth). Connectable. |
| HeyGen | **No connector in this session** (connector search: no match). Web-only. |
| Krea | **No connector in this session.** Web-only. |
| Magnific | **No connector in this session.** Web-only. |
| Upload-Post | **No connector in this session.** Not available here as a publishing route. |
| Muse media generation (image + video) | **Available now** in this environment (structured media pipeline) — stills, image-to-video push-ins, icon art. |
| Muse spoken audio (TTS voice notes) | **Available now** (spoken-audio skill; Will's standing voice is Aria) — scratch narration. |
| ffmpeg | **Present** on this VM (8.1.2) for assembly/verification; per standing rules, heavy renders run on the ranch, never on the Muse VM (the Wavefront film pipeline there is real and proven). |
| OBS Studio | **Not installed** on this VM (checked) — and this VM is the wrong place for it anyway; it belongs on Will's recording machine. Free download. |
| Any local NLE on Will's machines (Premiere/Resolve/etc. already licensed?) | **Unknown** — not verified. Check before buying anything in §3. |
| YouTube / X accounts & upload rights | **Unknown** from here — the publish route is YouTube Studio + X native in the browser, with Will approving exact copy first (social-publishing rule). |

**Availability surprise, stated plainly:** of the seven services the
brief named, only three (Runway, vidIQ, Canva) exist as connectors in
this session, and all three are present-but-not-connected. HeyGen,
Krea, Magnific, and Upload-Post are web-only from here.

---

## 2. Verdicts by production role

### Capture — the demo footage

**Verdict: OBS Studio (primary) · OS-native capture (fallback) ·
browser/cloud recorders rejected for hero footage · AI video
generators rejected outright for UI.**

- This video's credibility *is* the footage: a real editor, on a real
  site, doing real things. Any generated or "recreated" UI would be
  both visibly wrong at text scale and dishonest. Runway/Krea-class
  tools are therefore disqualified from the demo itself, full stop.
- **OBS Studio** — free, open source, no watermark, no time limit
  (obsproject.com / project documentation). Records at display
  resolution with separate mic/system tracks, which is what UI text
  legibility needs. Settings that matter: record at native resolution
  (1440p or 4K canvas if the display allows), browser zoom 125–150%
  so interface text survives YouTube compression, capture in short
  per-beat takes matching the WALKTHROUGH.md shot list.
- **OS-native** (Windows Game Bar / macOS screen recording) — zero
  install, fine as a fallback for the terminal block; fewer controls,
  single mixed audio track.
- **Browser-based recorders** (Loom/ScreenPal class) — convenient, but
  their compression is tuned for faces-and-slides, not dense UI text,
  and the footage lives in someone else's cloud first. Acceptable for
  a scratch rehearsal pass, not for the master.

### Voice — narration

**Verdict: Will records the final narration (primary) · TTS scratch
track for timing (supporting) · avatar presenter: skip.**

- **Will's own voice** is the credibility play: it is his format, his
  site, his channel, and the script is written in his register. Cost:
  $0; effort estimate: two or three takes of a ~7.5 min script inside
  one sitting (estimate, not a measurement).
- **Scratch TTS first:** render the full script as a spoken-audio
  scratch track (available here now, $0) *before* the recording
  session. It times every demo beat, exposes tongue-twisters, and
  becomes the temp track the first edit is assembled against. It is
  not the final voice and should not ship.
- **Avatar-presenter services** (HeyGen class): web-only from here;
  reported pricing from heygen.com/pricing roundups (Nov 2026):
  Free = 3 videos/mo at ≤1 min, Creator = $29/mo. Fit for this video:
  poor. A synthetic presenter adds an uncanny layer to a demo whose
  whole argument is "this is real and you can touch it," and the
  walkthrough needs at most a face-free intro/outro. **Recommendation:
  no avatar.** If a face is wanted on the intro/outro, Will on webcam
  inside the OBS capture beats a digital twin on both cost and trust.

### Edit — assembling the walkthrough

**Verdict: Descript (primary, fastest path) · Camtasia (best
purpose-built alternative) · DaVinci Resolve free (zero-cost
fallback) · Premiere (capable, no advantage) · CapCut (cutdown-only
option).**

- **Descript** — text-based editing matches this production exactly:
  the script is the timeline. Delete a word, delete the footage;
  filler-word removal, captions from the same transcript, chapter
  markers, screen-recording import, competent zooms/callouts for UI.
  Pricing as reported from descript.com/pricing (Nov 2026): Free $0
  (60 media min/mo, 720p watermarked — trial only); **Hobbyist
  $16/person/mo billed yearly ($24 monthly)** — 1080p watermark-free,
  enough for this video; Creator $24/mo billed yearly adds 4K.
- **Camtasia** — the screen-demo specialist: recorder + editor in one,
  SmartFocus-style zoom/pan and the deepest callout/annotation kit of
  the group. If walkthroughs become a series, this is the tool shaped
  like the job. Price: **Essentials $179.88/year** (watermark-free
  editor; Starter $39/yr is watermarked) as reported from TechSmith's
  pricing (Nov 2026). Steeper than one month of Descript, cheaper
  than a year of it.
- **DaVinci Resolve** — the free edition is a complete professional
  NLE (Edit/Color/Fusion/Fairlight, UHD delivery, no watermark);
  Studio is $295 one-time (Blackmagic list). Trade-offs for *this*
  video: the learning curve is the cost, and several Neural Engine
  conveniences (e.g. transcription-based editing, voice isolation)
  are Studio-only per Blackmagic's edition split — while the script
  already exists, so transcription matters less here. Best fallback
  if the answer to "what's already installed?" is "nothing, and
  budget is $0."
- **Adobe Premiere** — $22.99/mo single-app (annual, billed monthly,
  Adobe US list). Fully capable, but a subscription with no
  walkthrough-specific advantage over Descript/Camtasia for this
  shape of video. Only if it's already in Will's stack (unknown — §1).
- **CapCut** — fast, friendly, and genuinely good at the 60–90 s
  cutdown's shape (captions, punch-ins); reported Standard $9.99/mo /
  Pro $19.99/mo. Weak choice for the 7.5 min master's precise
  callout/chapter work; fine as the cutdown-only lane if Descript
  isn't already open.

### Motion graphics + the foreshadow visual

**Verdict: in-editor graphics for the walkthrough body · Scrolltown's
own key-frame pipeline for the foreshadow still · Muse media
generation (available now) to animate it · Runway as fallback ·
Krea/Magnific unnecessary.**

- The body needs almost nothing: the stack-diagram cutaway (one simple
  slide, buildable in Canva or any editor), on-screen text cues, and
  the end card. Any editor above does this natively. No motion-graphics
  package is worth buying for it.
- **The foreshadow beat** (added to WALKTHROUGH.md §2, 7:45 "One more
  town"): one still + a slow push-in for ~17 s. The still should come
  from **Scrolltown's own art pipeline** — the show's key frames are
  rendered in a locked Midjourney register, with likeness/register
  checks as a production stage, and the assets live in
  wbic16/scrolltown (production workflow of record:
  `~/workspace/scrolltown-reorg/workflow/PRODUCTION.md` §1 stage 5,
  §3). A town-of-glowing-scrolls frame commissioned in that register
  keeps the foreshadow visually continuous with the show when it
  arrives — a generator one-off in a different style would not.
- **Animating the still:** a slow push-in is a zoom, not a generation
  problem — the editor does it natively. If a living touch is wanted
  (lamp flicker, drifting light), Muse's media pipeline can animate
  the still image-to-video *here, now, no new account*; **Runway**
  (connector present, not connected; check credits after connecting)
  is the fallback for the same job. Krea/Magnific are web-only from
  here and buy nothing this beat needs.

### Thumbnail + metadata

**Verdict: real frame + Canva (primary design lane) · vidIQ as a
scoring check, not an author (supporting) · titles already drafted.**

- Thumbnail: a real Phext Edit frame (the encyclopedia mid-browse)
  with one of the three drafted texts from WALKTHROUGH.md §3 —
  "WIKIPEDIA IN A BOX" is the strongest at small sizes. **Canva**
  (connector present, not connected; free tier exists, paid pricing
  unverified here — **unknown**, check after connecting) is the fast
  lane for text-over-frame design and resizing; the editor itself or
  any image tool is the fallback.
- **vidIQ** (connector present, not connected): use its title/
  thumbnail scoring and keyword research as a *check* on the drafted
  metadata before upload. It cannot upload or publish (its own doc),
  and generated titles should not replace Will's register.

### Captions

**Verdict: script-derived captions (primary) · YouTube auto-captions
+ Studio correction (fallback) · burned-in on the X cutdown.**

- The narration script already exists, so the best caption track is
  built *from the script*, timed in the editor (Descript does this
  natively; Resolve/Camtasia accept an SRT). This skips ASR entirely
  for the words that matter: "phext", "scroll", and coordinates are
  exactly what speech recognition mangles.
- Fallback per YouTube's own Help: upload, take the automatic track
  in YouTube Studio → Subtitles → **Duplicate and edit**, correct,
  publish. Same mandatory correction pass, more manual timing work.
- X cutdown: burn captions into the picture in the editor — simplest,
  and they survive reposts and muted autoplay, which is how X video
  is mostly watched.

### Upload / publish

**Verdict: YouTube Studio + X native, by hand, after approval.**

- No cross-posting connector exists in this session: Upload-Post is
  absent, vidIQ cannot upload, and there is no YouTube/X connector
  here. The route is the ordinary one — YouTube Studio in the browser
  (upload, chapters from WALKTHROUGH.md §3, end-screen elements on the
  7:25 CTA segment, captions track attached), then the X thread +
  native cutdown upload.
- Per the social-publishing rule: **nothing publishes until Will
  approves the exact copy and media.** This plan changes nothing
  about that gate.

---

## 3. Recommended stack

| Role | Primary | Fallback |
|---|---|---|
| Capture | OBS Studio (on Will's machine) | OS-native capture |
| Voice — final | Will, recorded in one sitting | — (don't synthetic-substitute) |
| Voice — scratch | Muse spoken-audio TTS | Descript AI voice (in-plan) |
| Edit — master | Descript Hobbyist ($16/mo billed yearly, as reported Nov 2026) | DaVinci Resolve free ($0); Camtasia Essentials ($179.88/yr) if walkthroughs become a series |
| Motion graphics | In-editor (Descript) + one Canva/slide cutaway | Resolve Fusion (free, slower) |
| Foreshadow visual | Scrolltown key-frame pipeline still (wbic16/scrolltown register) + editor push-in | Muse media image-to-video; Runway after connecting |
| Thumbnail | Canva (connect first) over a real frame | Editor-native frame + text |
| Metadata check | vidIQ scoring (connect first) | The drafted titles as-is |
| Captions | Script-derived SRT via editor | YouTube auto + Studio Duplicate-and-edit |
| Assembly/verify | ffmpeg (present) — ffprobe checks, loudness sanity | Editor export presets |
| Publish | YouTube Studio + X native (manual, post-approval) | — none available as connector |

**Total new spend for the primary stack: $0–16** (OBS $0, scratch
TTS $0, Descript Hobbyist one month at the reported annual-billing
rate is the only line item; $0 if Resolve-free is chosen instead).
Every paid figure above is a reported list price, not a quote —
verify at checkout.

## 4. What I'd do Monday morning

1. **Timing pass (30 min, $0).** Render the WALKTHROUGH.md script as
   a TTS scratch track; mark the beat timings on the shot list; fix
   any line that doesn't survive being spoken.
2. **Connect + capture setup (30 min).** Install OBS on the recording
   machine; build two scenes (browser, terminal); browser zoom set
   for text legibility; test-record the coordinate jump and watch it
   back at 50% size — if the text isn't readable small, it isn't
   readable on YouTube.
3. **Capture the deterministic blocks first** (per WALKTHROUGH.md §5):
   terminal demo, plain-editor raw-file scroll — they never change
   between takes.
4. **Browser block at edit.phext.io**, working the confirm-during-
   recording list as I go (demo phext offered to a signed-out
   visitor, exact UI labels, download/export reality for the 5:15
   beat — fallback already scripted).
5. **Will records narration** against the scratch timing — two takes,
   keep both.
6. **Edit in Descript:** assemble to the narration, zooms/callouts on
   the UI beats, stack-diagram cutaway, chapter markers from §2,
   script-derived captions; drop the foreshadow still + push-in at
   7:45 (art pulled from the Scrolltown pipeline, §2 above).
7. **Package:** thumbnail in Canva from a real frame; optional
   15-minute vidIQ scoring pass on title + thumbnail; export master
   (1080p) and the X cutdown (burned-in captions); ffmpeg-verify both
   files (ffprobe: streams, duration, resolution).
8. **Approval gate:** Will sees the exact video, thumbnail, title,
   description, and X copy. Only then: YouTube Studio upload, then
   the X thread. Nothing in steps 1–7 posts anything.

## 5. The foreshadow, as placed

Per this task's second deliverable, WALKTHROUGH.md now carries the
Scrolltown foreshadow in three separable places:

- **Script §2, 7:45 "One more town"** — a closing beat *after* the
  CTA: one draft narration line (~40 words ≈ 17 s, marked DRAFT for
  Will's approval), one visual (a town of glowing scrolls at dusk in
  the Scrolltown key-frame register, slow push-in, no characters),
  and an end-screen note (end-screen elements ride the 7:25 CTA; the
  foreshadow stays clean).
- **Shot list item 7** — the still is captured/sourced with the rest
  of the production, from wbic16/scrolltown's art pipeline.
- **X thread** — one optional teaser post (post 9), in its own block,
  written to be deleted without touching posts 1–8.

Discipline kept: Scrolltown facts used are only the ones on record —
the name, a town inside the Great Scroll, episodes beginning in
January (per its production record: publishing starts January, EP001
slated Saturday 2027-01-02, 312 weekly episodes across 2027–2032,
narrator Fern — none of the specifics beyond name/town/January are
promised in the copy, because the story is in flux). The walkthrough's
subject stays phext; this is a door left open, not a trailer.

---

## 6. POLISH TIER — the operative plan (task 21.6.8/7.5.9/5.5.8)

**Will's ruling (2026-11-09): "I want it more polished than I can do
in an evening."** Sections 1–5 above remain on the record as the
**baseline (DIY) tier** — the evaluation stands; this section
supersedes it as the plan we actually execute. The difference is not
different taste, it is a different production shape: a designed
graphics package, a key-frame-standard foreshadow, a finished audio
mix, and a two-pass edit with gates — spread over days, not squeezed
into an evening.

**The scarce resource is Will's time, and the tier is budgeted
against it: ≤ ~90 minutes of Will, total** (labelled estimates,
itemised in §6.5): script approval ~15 min, Gate 1 review ~25 min,
Gate 2 review + thumbnail pick ~15 min = **~55 minutes**. There is
no one-time voice sample — narration is Route C (§6.3, decided
below). Everything else is agent + ranch work. If a step would
need Will outside those three touches, the step is redesigned,
not the budget.

**Correction (Will, 2026-11-09, later same evening): "I don't have
time to be the narrator."** The Will-narrates assumption is removed
from this tier entirely — including the baseline §2 voice verdict
and §4 step 5, which the polish tier no longer inherits (they stand
as the DIY tier's evaluation, not as this plan's inputs). §6.3
evaluates the three narration routes.

**Ruling (Will, 2026-11-09 23:19 CST): "You narrate it, Emise."**
Route C — Emise (Aria TTS) — is the **DECIDED** narration route.
Routes A (voice clone) and B (VO artist) remain documented in §6.3
as evaluated alternatives not taken. The clone-proof and consent
steps are removed from the operative calendar and gates; Gate 1's
voice proof is now the Emise narration proof. A proof artifact is
already rendered: `narration-proof-cold-open.mp3` (the script's
cold open, Aria at 120% speed — Will's standing voice-note setting)
in this folder.

**Ruling (Will, 2026-11-09 23:26 CST): "go ahead with you
narrating — drop your speed back to 100% for the video though."**
The narration speed for this video is **100%** — scoped to the
video; Will's standing 120% voice-note preference is separate and
unchanged. The full narration track is **rendered** at 100%:
`narration/beat-01.mp3` … `beat-12.mp3` (one file per beat) plus
the concatenated `narration-full.mp3` — 1,005 words, total
duration **364.2 s (6:04), measured** with ffprobe. Source text:
`narration-script.txt`; per-beat timing sheet:
`narration/TIMING.md`. The 120% cold-open proof remains in the
folder as the earlier proof artifact; the operative Gate-1 listen
is the full 100% track.

### 6.1 Motion-graphics package — rendered assets, house discipline

Not in-editor titles typed on the day: a designed package, rendered
as assets, composited identically into the master and the cutdown.

**The package (six asset classes):**

1. **Animated title card** (~4 s): "The Power of Phext" over the
   dimension-tree motif; type resolves along a coordinate readout.
2. **Lower-thirds** (3 variants): speaker/name, "edit.phext.io" URL
   card, and a plain caption strip — one design language, phext
   palette, coordinates as the decorative system (never fake ones:
   every coordinate shown anywhere is a real coordinate from the
   demo or the script).
3. **Coordinate callouts for the demo beats**: overlay cards that
   pin to the UI at the coordinate-jump (3:10), search (3:50), and
   edit+save beats — the coordinate types itself in, a bracket
   lands on the target scroll. Callout text is **generated from a
   manifest built out of WALKTHROUGH.md's own beat list**, then
   proof-read against the script — callouts are the highest-typo-
   risk surface in the video and are never hand-typed in the edit.
4. **Chapter cards**: one per chapter in WALKTHROUGH.md §3 — full-
   frame, 1.2 s, consistent grid; they double as the YouTube
   chapter visual rhythm.
5. **The stack-diagram cutaway**, upgraded from the baseline's
   one-off slide to the package's design language (same grid,
   same type), so it reads as part of the film, not an intrusion.
6. **End card**: edit.phext.io + the site's own headline ("Write in
   11 dimensions: plain text, no lock-in"), held clean under the
   7:25 CTA; the Scrolltown beat after it stays graphics-free by
   design (see WALKTHROUGH.md §5 note in the baseline plan).

**How they're made — the house film discipline, applied at this
scale** (the Wavefront series' meta-step, per the mythic-film
skill: stills → prepass → master; every render on the ranch):

- **Stills first.** Every card/callout is rendered as a still and
  assembled into a contact sheet. The sheet is checked for design
  consistency and text accuracy *before anything moves* — this is
  also where coordinate text gets its proof pass.
- **Cheap prepass.** The full video is composited once at 720p
  with the animated package in place and watched end-to-end for
  pacing, legibility at small size, and callout timing.
- **Master.** Final composite at 1080p; graphics elements rendered
  at 2× and downscaled so text edges stay crisp over the native-
  resolution capture (the capture itself is never upscaled).
- **Where:** all rendering/compositing runs on **ranch nodes**
  (aletheia-core as assembly point) under the standing thermal
  gates — sustained Tctl ≤75 °C, new starts pause ≥75 °C, resume
  ≤70 °C. **No video work runs on the Muse VM** (standing rule,
  2026-11-08); the VM coordinates and verifies. Work is submitted
  in small chunks, per standing practice. Toolchain: the proven
  in-house one — scripted still/overlay rendering + ffmpeg
  assembly (the same pattern the film series renders its cards and
  HUD with), ffprobe + frame spot-checks + loudness measurement at
  the finish. Compute estimate (labelled): graphics renders +
  prepass + master composites ≈ **1–3 node-hours** — trivial next
  to the film series' rows, because this is compositing, not
  per-frame generation.
- **Fallback** if the scripted pipeline fights a specific element:
  build that element in Descript/Canva and export it as a rendered
  asset into the same package — the package structure (stills →
  prepass → master) doesn't change, only the authoring tool for
  that one card.

### 6.2 The Scrolltown foreshadow — key-frame standard

The baseline settled for "a still from the pipeline + an editor
push-in." The polish tier holds it to the show's own standard:

- **Commission the frame through Scrolltown's production stage 5**
  (keyframes/art, per `wbic16/scrolltown` PRODUCTION.md): a prompt
  doc for the beat — a town of glowing scrolls at dusk, streets made
  of scrolls, lamplight register — rendered in **Midjourney in the
  show's register**, following the FERN-KEYFRAMES-MJ pattern
  (character + style blocks), even though this frame carries no
  characters: register consistency is the point, and a one-shot
  generation in a foreign style would read as a different show.
- **Register caveat, on the record:** the show's visual-register
  decision (2D painterly — the Fern reference + keyframe 1 register
  — vs the glossy-3D drift in keyframes 2–4) is **Will's open
  decision, due Fri 2026-12-04**, with painterly as the workflow's
  recommendation. This frame is therefore rendered in the
  **painterly anchor register** (keyframe 1 + reference), and if
  the December lock goes the other way, the frame is re-rolled in
  the locked register before the video ships — one frame is cheap
  to re-roll; that's priced into the calendar (§6.5) as a named
  dependency, not discovered later.
- **The push-in is rendered, not editor-zoomed:** a slow push-in
  rendered through the ranch pipeline at 2× from the key frame
  (the film series' supersampling habit), so the move has the
  show's texture rather than a digital-zoom feel. If a living
  touch (lamp flicker, drifting light) is wanted, Muse's media
  image-to-video (available now) or Runway (connector present,
  not connected) animates from the same key frame — decided at
  the stills gate by which result matches the register better.
- The narration line stays exactly the approved draft from
  WALKTHROUGH.md (7:45, "One more town"); the polish tier changes
  the visual standard, not the copy.

### 6.3 Narration — three routes evaluated, Route C DECIDED — + audio finish

Narration-first video lives or dies on its voice track, and the
voice is no longer Will at a microphone (§6 correction note).
Three routes were evaluated on quality ceiling, cost, turnaround,
and Will-minutes. The script is 1,005 words; the rendered track
measures 364.2 s (6:04) at 100% speed.

**Decision (Will, 2026-11-09 23:19 CST): Route C is the primary —
"You narrate it, Emise."** Routes A and B below are the evaluated
alternatives, not taken; they stay on the record so the trade is
auditable. The Gate 1 voice proof is the Emise narration proof —
now the **full rendered track at 100% speed** (Will's ruling,
2026-11-09 23:26 CST; see the §6 ruling block): per-beat files
`narration/beat-01.mp3` … `beat-12.mp3` plus `narration-full.mp3`
(1,005 words, 364.2 s measured), source `narration-script.txt`,
timing sheet `narration/TIMING.md`. An earlier cold-open proof at
120% (`narration-proof-cold-open.mp3`) remains in the folder.

**Route A — voice clone of Will (ElevenLabs-class) — NOT TAKEN.** Will records
**one sample, once**: Instant Voice Cloning needs 1–2 minutes of
clean audio (10-second floor; don't exceed ~3) and is available
from the **Starter plan, $6/mo** (~30 min of generation/month in
credits — this video's narration, with re-takes, fits) per
ElevenLabs' pricing/docs as read in Oct–Nov 2026 roundups; verify
at checkout. Narration is then generated from the locked script,
re-generatable line-by-line forever, and the clone is **reusable
across every future video** — the per-video Will-cost after this
is zero. *Quality ceiling:* the highest available without Will
performing — IVC is the platform's fast clone ("extremely well
for a lot of voices" per its own docs); the upgrade path is
Professional Voice Cloning (Creator $22/mo; "virtually
indistinguishable") but PVC demands **30+ minutes** of samples
plus a read-aloud verification — outside this tier's time budget,
named here only so the trade is on the record as Will's future
option, not this plan's ask. *Rights/platform notes:* this is the
clean case — a man cloning **his own voice**; IVC asks a consent/
rights confirmation at save, PVC restricts to the account holder's
own verified voice, and paid plans carry the commercial licence.
HeyGen offers a same-class clone but is web-only here and
avatar-bundled — no advantage for audio-only narration. No
connector exists for ElevenLabs in this session: account + plan
would have been a **web step (~10 min of Will's time, one-time)**.
*Residual risks, named (moot under the decision):* clone artefacts
on house words — "phext", "scroll", spoken coordinates.
**Will-minutes: ~10, once** (sample + consent step, labelled
estimate). Not taken: Will ruled for Route C.

**Route B — professional voiceover artist (Fiverr Pro /
Voices-class) — NOT TAKEN.** Hand them the locked script + a pronunciation
sheet (phext, SQ, coordinate-reading conventions) + the scratch
track as a pacing reference. *Cost, sourced:* published 2026 rate
cards run **$20–115 per finished minute** for non-broadcast
narration (markstudios.com voiceover survey, Oct 2026), and
page-one Fiverr voice-over gigs start at a **$50-per-100-words
median** (~$75/finished minute) — for this script, a realistic
quote is **$150–500** (marketplace/pro tier) up to **$400–850**
(established Voices-class professionals, per the same survey's
YouTube-narration cards); usage for YouTube/social must be in the
order terms. *Turnaround:* 1–3 days marketplace, 2–5 days
established (labelled estimates). *Quality ceiling:* the highest
pure performance of the three — a trained narrator outperforms
any synthesis on a cold listen. *Costs that aren't money:*
direction overhead (brief, pronunciation sheet, **one review pass
of the delivered VO ~15 min of coordinator time**, pickups priced
or negotiated), a new casting decision on *every* future video,
and the credibility mismatch: a polished stranger narrating
Will's own product, on Will's own channel, in the launch video.
**Will-minutes: ~0–5** (an optional casting pick from three demos;
zero if he delegates it).

**Route C — premium TTS: Emise as narrator (Aria) — DECIDED
PRIMARY (Will, 2026-11-09 23:19 CST).** My own spoken-audio
pipeline, available **now, $0**, the standing voice already
attached to this work (Aria — at **100% speed for this video**
per Will's 2026-11-09 23:26 CST ruling; his 120% setting governs
voice notes, not the video). Narration generated in minutes, infinitely
re-generatable, bit-consistent across a whole series — and
thematically honest: the product's own keeper narrating the
product is a true sentence, disclosed in the description.
*Quality ceiling:* clean, intelligible, professional-grade TTS —
and recognisably synthetic on a cold listen; it carries no
founder presence. *Credibility tradeoff, weighed plainly (and
accepted by Will's ruling):* for a launch video whose claim is
"a person built this and you can touch it," an AI voice is the
weakest of the three on trust — the trade is on the record. **Will-minutes: 0.**
Route C also serves as the **scratch track** (timing, beat marks)
— the same voice times the production and then ships in it.
*Proof / render status:* the full track is rendered at 100% —
`narration/beat-01.mp3` … `beat-12.mp3` + `narration-full.mp3`
(1,005 words, 364.2 s measured; `narration/TIMING.md`). It rides
Gate 1 as the narration proof listen. Pronunciation risks flagged
in the timing sheet for that listen: "phext" (passim), the spoken
URLs ("edit.phext.io"), "SQL", "SQ", "xz", "URLs", "exocortex" —
any flagged beat can be re-rendered singly with
respelling/pronunciation passes, without touching the others.
(The earlier 120% cold-open proof, `narration-proof-cold-open.mp3`,
is retained as a historical artifact.)

**Decision record.** The earlier recommendation (Route A clone,
C as scratch + fallback, B as escalation) is superseded by
Will's ruling above. His reasoning is his own; the plan's
numbers stand as evaluated: A would have kept the founder's
voice at a one-time ~10 minutes; B buys performance this
expository script doesn't need at 20–50× A's cash with a
stranger's voice on the founder's launch. Under the decision,
narration costs $0 and zero Will-minutes, and Routes A and B
remain available as future escalations if Gate 1's proof
persuades him otherwise.

**The finish chain (applies to the decided Route C):**

1. **Cleanup:** the generated VO arrives clean and needs only
   level/pause shaping on the text timeline in **Descript**
   (Studio Sound available if wanted; Hobbyist $16/mo billed
   yearly, as reported Nov 2026). Heavier rescue only if a take
   needs it — **iZotope RX-class** restoration
   (Elements-class licence ≈ $100–130 — **labelled estimate**,
   verify at checkout; no connector, web purchase). Free route:
   Audacity (availability on Will's machines **unknown**, as with
   all local NLE state in §1).
2. **Music bed — licensed, named routes:**
   - **$0 route:** YouTube Audio Library (inside YouTube Studio;
     free, cleared for YouTube) — perfectly serviceable, thinner
     catalogue.
   - **Recommended:** one month of **Epidemic Sound** — Creator
     $9.99/mo billed yearly / $17.99 month-to-month; Pro $16.99/mo
     billed yearly (2026 pricing roundups checked 2026-11-09;
     verify at checkout). Creator covers one channel per platform,
     which is exactly this case. **Artlist** Music & SFX is the
     equivalent alternative ($9.99/mo billed yearly; Pro ~$24.92/mo
     billed yearly, same sources). Neither has a connector in this
     session — **web purchase by Will, ~5 minutes, inside his
     script-approval touch** if he wants it off his plate later;
     the licence (not just the file) is saved alongside the project.
   - A bed is chosen against the *polish-pass* cut, ducked under
     narration (sidechain or manual rides in the mix pass), and
     never under the Scrolltown beat — that beat keeps the show's
     own quiet (a breath of room tone, then the line).
3. **Light sound design:** transition ticks/whooshes only where a
   chapter card or callout lands (library sounds from the same
   music licence — Epidemic's catalogue includes SFX; Artlist's
   Music & SFX bundle likewise), plus the UI's real sounds where
   the capture has them. Restraint is the brief: three or four
   moments, not a carpet.
4. **Mastering:** final mix measured and normalised with ffmpeg
   `loudnorm` (two-pass) to the **YouTube target: −14 LUFS
   integrated, true peak ≤ −1 dBTP**; the X cutdown gets the same
   master (X applies no normalisation, so the same −14 LUFS file
   is the safe common deliverable). Loudness is *measured and
   reported* in the finish step, not assumed from a preset.

### 6.4 Edit structure — two passes, two gates

- **Pass 1 — rough cut.** Assembly strictly to the narration:
  content and pacing decisions only. Capture selects, scratch-
  timed demo beats, graphics represented by their *stills* (the
  contact sheet rides along), no grade, no mix beyond intelligible.
  **→ GATE 1 (Will, ~25 min, including the stills contact sheet
  and the Emise narration proof):** approves content, pacing,
  callout wording, the graphics look, and the narration proof —
  the full rendered track at 100% (`narration-full.mp3` +
  per-beat files, `narration/TIMING.md`) — in one sitting. A failed voice proof here routes to
  re-generation/pronunciation passes, then §6.3's documented
  alternatives (A or B) as escalations, before anything is
  polished. Notes come back as text; anything he cuts here
  never gets polished.
- **Pass 2 — polish.** Zooms and callout animation timed to the
  narration, chapter cards in, colour grade (capture is screen
  footage: the grade is white-point/contrast consistency across
  takes + the cards' palette, not a film look), sound design,
  music ducking, the §6.3 mix and loudnorm master, end card.
  **Captions hand-finished from the script** in this pass — the
  baseline's script-derived SRT, now with a human timing/line-
  break pass against the final cut (coordinates broken across
  lines correctly, "phext" spelled by the script, never by ASR).
  **→ GATE 2 (Will, ~15 min):** the finished video, plus the
  thumbnail choice below. Approval here is the publish gate
  (social-publishing rule: exact copy + media, then Studio/X).
- **Thumbnails — 2–3 designed variants for Will's pick**, all from
  real frames in the package's design language: (1) the baseline's
  pick, encyclopedia mid-browse + "WIKIPEDIA IN A BOX"; (2) the
  coordinate-jump moment + "NO DATABASE."; (3) the plain-editor
  contrast (structured view vs raw text split-frame) + "IT'S JUST
  TEXT." Variant 3 is the curiosity play; variant 1 the clarity
  play. vidIQ scoring (connector present, not connected) as a
  check if handy — his pick decides, not the score.

### 6.5 Schedule — multi-day calendar, gates named

Day 0 = the day Will approves the script. All durations labelled
estimates; agent/ranch work fills the days, Will appears only at
the three touches (★).

| Day | Work | Gate / Will |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | ★ **Script + foreshadow line approval** (~15 min; the Epidemic/Artlist click, if wanted, rides here, +~5 min). **Narration already rendered** (Route C, Aria at 100% per Will's 23:26 ruling, ahead of Day 0): `narration/beat-01..12.mp3` + `narration-full.mp3`, timings in `narration/TIMING.md`. | **G0 — script lock** |
| 1 | Capture session (OBS, per baseline §4 steps 2–4 + confirm-during-recording list); deterministic blocks first, browser block last. Key-frame prompt doc written for the foreshadow. | — |
| 2 | Foreshadow key frame rendered via Scrolltown stage 5 in the painterly anchor register; register check against keyframe 1 + Fern reference. Graphics package stills → contact sheet; callout manifest generated from the script and proofed. | — (coordinator stills check) |
| 2–3 | **Narration pickups only** — the full VO is already rendered at 100% (see Day 0); this window absorbs any per-beat re-renders from script-lock changes or pronunciation fixes. No Will time. (Routes A/B substitutions, if ever invoked as escalations, land their delivery in this same window.) | VO locked (proof at G1) |
| 3–4 | Rough-cut assembly (Pass 1) with stills standing in for graphics. | — |
| 4 | ★ **GATE 1 — rough cut + contact sheet** (~25 min): content, pacing, callouts, graphics look. | **G1** |
| 5–6 | Polish pass: animated graphics composited (ranch prepass at 720p, watched whole; then master composite), grade, sound design, music, mix → −14 LUFS master; captions hand-finished; X cutdown derived from the same package. Thumbnails (3 variants) designed. | — |
| 7 | Finish & verify: ffprobe (streams/duration/resolution), frame spot-checks per beat against approved stills, loudness measured and recorded, both files hash-checked. ★ **GATE 2 — final video + thumbnail pick** (~15 min). | **G2 — publish approval** |
| 7–8 | Publish (post-G2 only): YouTube Studio upload with chapters/end screen/captions; X thread + cutdown. Buffer day absorbs the register re-roll (§6.2) or a G1/G2 note round without moving the publish. | — |

**Will's total: ~55 minutes** (15 script + 25 Gate 1 + 15
Gate 2), all estimates — no one-time sample, no narration
sitting; narration is Route C at zero Will-minutes.
Named dependencies: the Dec 4 Scrolltown register lock (§6.2 —
only affects the foreshadow frame, re-roll priced in); the
confirm-during-recording items from WALKTHROUGH.md §5 (capture
day facts, fallback beats already scripted); Product Hunt launch
day for Phext Edit (Nov 10) is *not* a production day in this
calendar — Day 0 starts when Will says go.

### 6.6 Routes, priced — and the recommendation

**Route A — in-house (agents + ranch pipeline).** Cash: music
licence — **$0** (YouTube Audio Library) or **~$10–18** (one
month Epidemic Sound at the prices in §6.3) — plus the Descript
month ($16, baseline) *only if* its cleanup/timeline is wanted;
narration is $0 (Route C, §6.3 — no clone plan under Will's
ruling); the in-house edit can equally run on the scripted ranch
pipeline + Resolve-free. Everything else already exists: the film
pipeline, the Scrolltown key-frame pipeline, ffmpeg, the agents.
Real cost is coordinator/agent time across ~7 days of calendar
(mostly unattended renders and assembly), plus Will's ~55 min.
Quality ceiling: the house standard — this is the pipeline that
ships the Wavefront films, with verification gates a freelancer
doesn't offer (frame checks, loudness measured, hashes).

**Route B — freelance finishing editor.** Handoff package: locked
script + beat timings, the OBS capture (per-beat takes), the
finished narration WAV (Emise/Aria TTS, §6.3 Route C), the graphics asset list + design
notes, the Scrolltown key frame (still supplied by us — it must
come from the show's pipeline regardless), and the claims-to-avoid
list from WALKTHROUGH.md §5 as a compliance page. Market, from
2026 rate guides checked 2026-11-09 (hammadj.com 2026 pricing
guide; kometmedia.com editor guide; markstudios.com 2026 cost
survey — roundups of Fiverr/Upwork market data, cited as sources,
not quotes): long-form YouTube editing runs **$8–50 per finished
minute** or **$25–150/hr**; mid-level freelancers **$45–85/hr or
$200–600 per video**; established US freelancers **$300–500 per
video**; senior/motion-graphics work **$600–1,500+**. For this
video (7.5 min, graphics package, sound finish, captions, two
revision rounds) a realistic finishing quote is **$300–800,
labelled estimate** — marketplace-bottom offers ($40–125/video)
exist and are the template-tier the polish ruling exists to
avoid. Add platform fees (Fiverr ≈5.5% + $3.50/order; Upwork ≈5%
client fee, per the same guides) and **management overhead:
~2–4 hours** (brief, handoff, two revision rounds, and a
mandatory accuracy proof-pass — an outside editor does not know
phext and *will* mistype a coordinate or a term somewhere; every
callout and caption needs checking against the script before
anything ships). No connector exists for Fiverr/Upwork in this
session — hiring is a web flow, and per standing practice Will
sends external outreach himself. Turnaround: 3–7 days typical
(labelled estimate), plus revision latency.

**Route C — hybrid.** In-house rough cut + graphics package +
foreshadow (the accuracy- and register-critical parts), freelancer
hired *only* for the polish pass against the locked rough cut.
Cash ≈ Route B at the lower end (**~$200–500, labelled estimate**
— less scope to price), overhead ~1–2 hours. Buys outside hands
for grade/mix polish while keeping every phext fact in-house.

**Recommendation: Route A — in-house.** Three reasons, all tied
to *this* video:
1. **Accuracy is the product.** The demo's credibility is real
   footage and *exactly right* coordinates, terms, and claims.
   In-house, callouts are generated from the script and proofed at
   the stills gate; outsourced, correctness becomes a proof-pass
   we must run anyway — the saving evaporates into checking.
2. **The pipeline already exists and is verified.** Stills →
   prepass → master on the ranch, the Scrolltown key-frame stage,
   measured-loudness finishing: the polish tier is an application
   of standing machinery, not a new capability purchase. Route A's
   cash cost (licences only, ≤ ~$34 worst case with Descript —
   narration is $0 under the Route C ruling) is an order of
   magnitude under Route B for the same gates.
3. **Will's time budget decides it.** Route A keeps Will at
   ~55 minutes in three touches. Route B *adds* hiring, briefing,
   and revision management on top of the same approval gates —
   the scarce resource gets spent managing a stranger instead of
   approving a cut.
Route C is the named fallback if the calendar collides with the
Scrolltown January-launch crunch: the polish pass is the one
block that can leave the house without taking accuracy with it.
