## Deviations and corrections, stated first

1. **T7-25 FAILED on the first master built from these pieces, and
   was fixed before delivery.** Frame grabs from the first master
   (129,967,378 B, SHA-256 `46ac05c6…`, superseded and not
   delivered) at push-in start (426.03 s), midpoint (447.73 s), and
   end (469.40 s) all showed the CTA headline as "Find it. Change
   it. Link it. Keep" — the "it." of "Keep it." off the right edge
   at every point of the push-in. This is the R4/R5/R6 defect,
   carried in the banked CTA stills. Root cause, in
   `render-cta-r7.py`: the headline was drawn at Bookman 92 starting
   at x=330; the four phrases measure 1,731 px, so the line ran to
   x=2,061 on a 1,920 px canvas and "it." was clipped off-canvas
   *before* the 0.86 downscale reframe — scaling cannot restore
   clipped ink. The renderer's own margin check measured only the
   ink that survived composition, so it reported a false PASS; two
   further checks downstream (a coordinator's look at the card, and
   this audit's first frame extraction) also missed it, because
   each looked at numbers or at the downscaled frame without
   reading the headline. Fix (`render-cta-r7-fix.py`, same art and
   method otherwise): the headline is measured, centered (x0=94 at
   the same 92 pt), and asserted inside the canvas at construction;
   the ink check then measures real ink (final frame x 217..1703 —
   ≥188 px of margin at the deepest zoom). The fixed source still
   was read by eye ("Keep it." complete), the CTA chain
   (cta-draw → cta-hold → §08 → slotfix → film join) was rebuilt
   from the fixed stills with everything else banked untouched, the
   film was re-mastered from the unchanged mix, and frames at
   push-in start, midpoint, and end were extracted from the final
   encode and read: the headline is complete at all three points.
   **T7-25 passes on the delivered master, verified by eye on the
   final encode** — the check whose absence let the first master
   through. Lesson, for V8: a cropped-characters check must assert
   against the composed line, never against surviving ink; and an
   extracted frame is not an audited frame until someone reads it.
2. **§05 swell +3.02 dB vs the ≤ +3.00 dB rule** (standing, from the
   mix stage; carried verbatim from `MIX-R7.md`): "RESIDUAL
   DEVIATION, stated plainly: §05 swell after the trim is +3.02 dB
   — still 0.02 dB over the ≤ +3.00 dB rule. Both permitted passes
   were used; per the brief the trim was not iterated further. The
   remaining excess is at the measurement's own rounding edge
   (values are reported to 0.01 dB), but as measured it does not
   pass." (Before trim +3.38 dB; max cut applied 0.443 dB of the
   0.8 dB cap; film 182.82–187.77 s. §05's integrated level stays in
   family; section spread 0.39 dB before and after.)
3. **Bed under voice, two measurement methods** (standing, from the
   mix stage, re-measured at acceptance with identical results).
   The brief's fixed-window method (narration vs ducked bed,
   voice_start+1 s .. +9 s) reads: agenda 9.14, §01 13.45, §02
   20.24, §03 **4.17**, §04 6.25, §05 8.24, §06 18.59, §07 17.59,
   §08 12.96 dB — 4 of 9 below the ≥10 dB target (min §03 4.17,
   median 12.96). A supplementary in-speech probe (longest
   continuous speech span per unit) reads: agenda **8.93**, §01
   21.55, §02 14.94, §03 19.93, §04 16.52, §05 15.58, §06 19.10,
   §07 17.52, §08 19.09 dB — only the agenda stays below 10 dB
   in-speech (min 8.93, median 17.52). Cross-checks reproduce the
   mix stage's own probes (§03 97.5–103.0 s: 19.8 dB; §04
   119.9–124.9 s: 9.67 dB). Cause, per the mix record: the mix
   follows the brief exactly (R5's duck law unchanged — 0.05
   threshold, 450 ms release), so this is a property of the law
   meeting R7's prosody: the audit windows contain chunk-internal
   pauses where the follower lets the bed rise before the next
   phrase. Not re-litigated at acceptance; retuning the duck law
   was mix-stage scope.
4. **T7-14: six unmarked silences >0.8 s inside §06's travel
   passage.** All 18 marked inter-chunk pauses are present on the
   stem (chunk gaps in the timing record equal the marked list
   exactly, 0.6 s each, §01 also 1.2 s; silencedetect aligns 12
   exactly, the other 6 contained in longer detected silences of
   1.12–1.21 s where a marked pause merges with an adjacent chunk-
   internal gap). All three biggest-claim pauses measure ≥1.0 s
   (§01: 1.2 s after "Nothing. It's still here."; §07 trailing
   15.7167 s; §08 trailing 22.3813 s). But §06 chunk 3
   (231.77–263.13 s) contains six silences of 0.83, 0.86, 1.12,
   1.40, 0.85, 0.85 s (spans 235.93–236.76, 240.22–241.08,
   246.12–247.24, 247.92–249.32, 250.07–250.93, 254.08–254.93) that
   no `[pause]` token marks — Aria's pauses between the hop lines.
   They are covered by hop SFX in the mix and silent on the stem.
   A deviation under the rule's letter ("no unmarked silence
   >0.8 s inside a section").
5. **T7-24: the final beat's planned slow rate was not realized in
   the recorded stem.** The script's slot plan called for §08's
   last two sentences (~31 words) at ≤130 wpm across a ≥13.8 s
   slot — planned as the slowest beat in the film. As recorded,
   the last chunk (440.74–447.05 s, 23 words) runs at 218.6 wpm
   chunk rate and the two-sentence beat at ≈190 wpm by speech
   span. The rule's other clauses pass: the consequence sentence
   ("Yours will fit in one file too — and if you keep that file,
   your knowledge stays with you, wherever you keep it.") is the
   last narration before the CTA card, nothing new follows it, and
   it ends on the film's smallest image. Mitigations on the
   record: §08 is the slowest section in the film by finished rate
   (128.0 wpm, every other unit 140.0), and the consequence
   sentence is followed by the film's longest designed silence —
   the 22.38 s CTA hold. The script audit's strict-reading note
   also stands: §08 contains spoken invitation lines before the
   consequence sentence, so under the reading "last narration
   before the spoken invitation" the beat in that position is an
   instruction, not a consequence; the script was built to the
   CTA-card reading, and both readings are recorded in
   `narration-r7/SCRIPT-AUDIT.md` §11 for a ruling.
6. **T7-09: §06's speech fill exceeds the 85% cap under the
   speech-seconds definition.** Fill = speech seconds ÷ section
   window, from the timing record: agenda 71.8%, §01 82.5%, §02
   74.5%, §03 66.3%, §04 70.7%, §05 73.1%, **§06 89.6%**, §07
   78.2%, §08 67.9% — all ≥50%; §06 alone over the cap, by 4.6
   points. §06's overage is structural: its travel passage is long
   chunks with few planted pauses (its speech-span rate, 153.5
   wpm, is the second-slowest in the film), and its slot-basis
   finished rate is exactly 140.0 wpm like every other unit; the
   assembly record notes no unit had negative slack and no pause
   was reduced. The rule's film-level clause passes outright:
   24.6% of the runtime is silence and picture-only beats
   (≥15% required).
7. **Script-audit flags carried, not hidden** (from
   `narration-r7/SCRIPT-AUDIT.md` §14): T7-10's strict reading is
   partial — the mandated film order plays the agenda card before
   §01, so the replaced picture lands in the first teaching
   section rather than before any agenda; the two hook questions
   are met in full. T7-20: "Wikipedia" (a proper name) and "fleet"
   (its metaphor noun) are first spoken in the zoom window's
   closing line — neither is a ladder concept; if the rule is
   extended to proper names, that one line is the exception.
   T7-05: "millions" has no countable restatement of the figure
   itself (everything around it is counted on screen). The
   agenda's largest-number pause job has no instance, because the
   agenda speaks no quantity — stated there rather than padded.
8. **T7-07 / T7-18 content density: carried from the plan, not
   recounted at acceptance.** The thematic-unit inventory behind
   the density clauses was fixed at planning (script audit §§2–3
   carry the throughline and one-idea tables); no unit inventory
   survives in the plan artifacts to recount against, and none was
   invented at acceptance to make the rules pass. The measurable
   finished-film correlates are in family and tabled below:
   per-unit finished rates (140.0 wpm everywhere, §08 128.0),
   fills (deviation 6), and §08 the film's slowest, least-filled
   teaching section after §03.

## How it was built (audit trail)

- **Script:** `SCRIPT-T7.md` — R4's narration revised, not
  rewritten: 1,067 words (whitespace count; ≈1,094 on R4's
  tokenisation), 27 `[pause]` tokens, throughline "One small file
  can hold, organize, connect, and share knowledge you keep."
  Script-level audit: `narration-r7/SCRIPT-AUDIT.md`.
- **Stem:** new Aria narration (the R4–R6 stem identity ends here,
  per D1), chunked per the slot table at a 140.0 wpm slot rate
  (§08 128.0), assembled by `assemble-r7.py` to
  `narr-stem-r7.wav` / timing record `timing-r7.json`: 1,067 words,
  353.88 s of speech, 11.4 s of marked pauses, finished rate
  136.4 wpm over the 469.4333 s stem.
- **Picture:** all sections re-rendered on aletheia-core under the
  thermal law (starts ≤70 °C Tctl, gates between pieces) — banked
  demo plates and renders reused per the brief; every section
  slotfixed to its exact slot count from *decoded* frames (the R6
  lesson), then joined with 0.4 s dissolves. Pre-join audit
  (decoded frames vs slot): title 480/480, agenda 1311/1311,
  §01 1774/1774, §02 1851/1851, §03 1594/1594, §04 2031/2031,
  §05 2571/2571, §06 4551/4551, §07 6763/6763, §08 5456/5456 —
  sum 28,382 − 9×24 dissolve frames = 28,166 nominal (28,167
  decoded in the final encode, +1). One upstream correction
  inside the join: the agenda body decoded 975 frames against a
  977 nominal (its internal xfades again); the 2-frame gap was
  absorbed by the assembler's clone-pad inside the agenda's tail
  hold, as designed — no narration timing moves.
- **Landings** (picture events placed against the stem, from the
  join audit): §02 landing −0.004 s, §03 +0.013 s of the 1.867 s
  targets; §06 hops −0.006 / −0.001 / −0.007 / +0.002 s (hop 3's
  window stretched from 1.600 s of source to 1.867 s for
  legibility, as ruled in the plan); §08 final hop (CTA hold
  start) +0.010 s. All inside ±0.1 s.
- **Mix:** narration + R5-recipe SFX re-locked to R7 timings
  (32 events, 0 boundary crossings; the 8 recipe WAVs
  hash-identical to the R5 set — `verify_r7.py`:
  SFX_SET_COMPLETE=True) + the music bed under R5's duck law with
  the §05 swell trim (deviation 2). Record: `MIX-R7.md`.
- **Master:** loudnorm (I=−14 / TP=−2 / LRA=11, measured-params
  linear) from the pre-master mix, muxed with faststart.
- **Thermal note:** all encode/mix work ran on aletheia-core under
  the 70/75 °C gate law. One sensor sample during the mix stage's
  FFT trim passes spiked to 87.1 °C; the mix render itself was
  held to a 70.0 °C start. No video work ran on this VM.

