# Phext Notepad — libphext v0.5.0 parity

Task coordinate 36.12.9/14.14.18/13.11.15 · Branch `emise/ultra-open-fix` · 2026-11-08
Commit **`636989ea0132bf2c8ab8732bdf57a23b33d67909`** on top of `2c75b8d` (the ultra-open fix),
pushed via aletheia-core SSH as wbic16 — fast-forward `2c75b8d..636989e`, force disabled,
remote ref re-verified via the GitHub API at `636989e`. No new branch, no PR, nothing merged.
All builds/tests ran on aletheia-core (cargo 1.94.0, .NET SDK 8.0.427, system liblzma 5.4.5).

## Verdict up front

The native app is at libphext 0.5.0 parity. The 1000-component cap that collapsed
ultra.phext to 41,963 of 320,958 scrolls is gone end to end: **ultra.phext now loads at
full fidelity — 320,958 scrolls — and serializes byte-identically to the source file**
(SHA-256 `fd68c5b0fd93a7388516fc20ae248f7fb16ebe2b1bb9b9b78d2ceedf8da8ac93`). Binary
scrolls and the xz seal are implemented in the core and proven byte-identical against the
real libphext-rs 0.5.0 crate with a mirror harness. The WinForms app needed **no code
change**: its parity obligation is round-trip preservation of binary scrolls, proven
headlessly (below). No libphext repo was bumped, tagged, or republished — the freeze binds
the libraries, and none was touched.

## Audit — where 0.3.1 semantics lived

**Native (Rust/FLTK).** `native/core` depended on `libphext = "0.3.1"` from crates.io for
the `Coordinate` type, break constants, and break methods; parsing/serialization were
already the app's own single-pass `parse.rs` from the perf pass. The 1000 cap entered in
three places:

1. **The dependency itself.** libphext 0.3.1's `Coordinate::*_break()` saturate at
   `COORDINATE_MAXIMUM = 1000`. `parse::explode` advances its walk coordinate with those
   methods, so every component pinned at 1000 and scrolls collided in the map — the
   ultra-open report's 41,963/320,958.
2. **`document::parse_coordinate`** rejected any component above 1000, so the jump box
   could not address most of ultra even where scrolls survived.
3. **`nav::CoordinateNav`** refused forward steps at 1000 and clamped `set_dimension`
   to 1000, capping the steppers the same way.

The FLTK app (`native/app`) has **no binary or seal surface at all** — open/save are
plain phext only, and `Document::load_path` rejects non-UTF-8 files. Seal therefore went
into the core layer with tests and **no UI was added or invented** (scope call per brief).

**WinForms (C#).** Its vendored model (post ultra-open fix) already has `int`
coordinates and needs nothing for coordinate parity. It has no binary API and no byte
ingestion path: it opens text files (`File.ReadAllText`) and stores scrolls as strings.
A canonical binary scroll *is* text (header line + base64, pure ASCII), so it can neither
be mis-parsed as structure nor mangled by the model — the parity question for this app is
exactly round-trip preservation, including at coordinates past its old `short` wrap.
**Scope call: no WinForms code changes; preservation proven by harness (below).** If the
WinForms app ever needs to *create* or *decode* binary scrolls, the parity surface already
exists for it in libphext-cs 0.5.0's `BinaryScroll` / `Phext.Seal`.

## Dependency decision

crates.io tops out at libphext **0.4.0** — that has unbounded coordinates but *no* binary
scrolls and *no* seal, so it is not parity. The core now pins the reference crate by git
rev, with the `binary` and `xz` features enabled:

```toml
libphext = { git = "https://github.com/wbic16/libphext-rs", rev = "e85d8b5676c6461e6bad6a89fe7a3d2eb06f552d", features = ["binary", "xz"] }
```

`e85d8b5` was verified via the GitHub API as libphext-rs `exo` HEAD at build time — the
PR #9 merge carrying version 0.5.0. The committed `Cargo.lock` records the same rev, so a
fresh clone reproduces the build bit-for-bit. Alternatives rejected: vendoring the crate
into the repo (duplicates upstream and drifts), and an in-app `Coordinate` type (would
fork document/nav/zoom away from the reference and still leave binary/seal to write).
**Residual for Will:** when he publishes libphext 0.5.0 to crates.io, this pin should be
swapped for `version = "0.5.0"` — a one-line change, deliberately left for that day.

## What changed (commit `636989e`, 10 files, +402/−21)

- `native/core/Cargo.toml` — the git pin + features (rationale in a comment).
- `native/Cargo.lock` — regenerated: libphext 0.5.0 from git, plus base64, xz2,
  liblzma-sys and their transitive deps.
- `native/core/src/nav.rs` — forward steps no longer refuse at a maximum (there is
  none); `set_dimension` clamps only the floor of 1.
- `native/core/src/document.rs` — `parse_coordinate` accepts any component ≥ 1; the
  jump error text no longer cites 1000. New Document idiom: `scroll_bytes()`
  (fetch-binary semantics at the current coordinate), `set_scroll_bytes()` (the
  ingestion rule), `set_binary_scroll()` (unconditional), `from_bytes()` (ingest path
  for foreign byte streams), `from_sealed()` / `seal()`.
- `native/core/src/binary.rs` — **new.** The canonical string-level surface
  (`is_binary_scroll`, `encode_scroll`, `decode_scroll`, `insert_binary`,
  `replace_binary`, `fetch_binary`, `insert_bytes`, `replace_bytes`,
  `phokenize_bytes`, `ingest`, `BINARY_SCROLL_HEADER`), re-exporting the reference
  implementation so the semantics are canonical by construction, plus 8 tests ported
  in substance from rs `test_binary.rs`.
- `native/core/src/seal.rs` — **new.** `seal` / `seal_bytes` / `unseal` /
  `unseal_string` / `SEAL_PRESET` (= 6), re-exported the same way, plus 2 tests.
- `native/core/src/lib.rs`, `parse.rs` — module wiring and doc updates (coordinate
  semantics are the reference crate's; the walk is now unbounded by construction).
- `native/README.md`, `docs/native-publishing.md` — the stale present-tense
  "libphext 0.3.1 … coordinate limits" descriptions corrected.
- The FLTK app crate is untouched, and still compiles (`cargo check -p phext-notepad`).

## Verification (all measured on aletheia-core)

**Test suites**
- Core suite on the exact pushed tree (`~/push-notepad` at `636989e`):
  **37 passed, 0 failed** — the perf branch's 23 baseline + 8 binary + 2 seal +
  4 new document tests (past-the-caps navigation/round-trip, binary round-trip through
  the Document, foreign-stream ingest, seal round-trip).
- WinForms ultra-open harness re-run against this branch's (unchanged) C# sources:
  **43/43 checks pass** — nothing regressed.

**Ultra full fidelity** (`agi_bench` + a load-only bench, same binary family)
- scrolls loaded: **320,958** (was 41,963); load matches `libphext::explode`
  (empties filtered): **true**; serialize matches `libphext::implode`: **true**;
  `serialize == input bytes`: **true**; fixed point: **true**.
- Spot checks: jump to `9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.223854` (ultra's maximum scroll) works and
  views its scroll; unit tests pin jumps/materialization at 1001, 40001, and 223854.

**Parity harness** (mirror of the libphext-cs interop approach; harness kept at
`~/workspace/phext-notepad-v050/harness/`, bins `refbin` = libphext 0.5.0 itself,
`corebin` = the notepad core's own Document API + its own serializer). One construction
script — the cs script's doc (plain/non-ASCII text, all-256-byte binary, empty-payload
binary, break-byte payload via byte insert, UTF-8 via byte replace, **1 MiB + 13 payload
at scroll 1006**, insert-then-replace pair, malformed binary, text-inserted
header+base64 namespace claim) plus an equal extension past every cap (binary at scroll
40001, text at `9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.223854`) — and one ingest stream:

| Check | Result |
|---|---|
| Constructed doc, ref vs core | **byte-identical** (SHA-256 `8647c69bcb1468f1…`) |
| Ingest output, ref vs core | **byte-identical** |
| Per-scroll manifest (fetch text + fetch_binary) | **identical** |
| Decode-verdict battery (41 candidates, the cs list verbatim) | **identical** |
| 1 MiB + 13 payload fetched at 1.1.1006 | **byte-identical** |
| Seals of agi / cyoa / doc / empty / small, ref vs core | **byte-identical, all five** |
| Cross-unseal both directions (agi, cyoa, doc) | **PASS**, exact originals |
| System `xz -dc` on the core's doc seal | exact original |
| Core unseals foreign seals: xz CLI preset 0, 9e + SHA256, 6 + no-check, 3 + CRC32 | exact originals — "any valid xz stream" holds |

One honesty note: `binary`/`seal` in the core re-export the reference functions, so
decode/seal agreement is by construction; the harness's independent weight sits in what
is *not* shared — the core's own parser, serializer, coordinate parsing, and Document
wiring, which produced the identical doc, ingest, and manifest bytes from the other side.

**WinForms binary preservation** (new harness, `~/workspace/phext-notepad-v050/winforms-binary/`,
compiling the branch's actual model sources): a canonical file holding a plain scroll,
an all-256-byte binary scroll, an empty-payload binary scroll, a malformed binary
scroll, a header-claim text scroll, and a binary scroll at `1.1.1/1.1.1/1.1.40001` —
**9/9 pass**: `Serialize()` round-trips byte-identically (40,501 chars), all 6 scrolls
held at their true coordinates with stored text intact, direct fetch at 40001 verbatim.

**Corpus regressions** (`agi_bench` equivalence legs): agi.phext (7,452 scrolls),
singular.phext (6,799), CYOA (830) — load matches explode, serialize matches implode,
round-trip and fixed point all **true**, as before.

**Performance guard** (ranch, same machine, load-only bench, median of 3)

| ultra.phext | Before (`2c75b8d`) | After (`636989e`) |
|---|---|---|
| Load | 74.0 ms | **166.2 ms** (agi_bench: 159.0 ms) |
| Scrolls held | 41,963 / 320,958 | **320,958 / 320,958** |
| Serialized bytes | 16,070,807 (≠ input) | **37,953,029 (= input)** |
| Peak RSS after load | 101,644 KiB | **238,396 KiB** |

Read this honestly: the "before" figures are the cost of the *collapsed* document —
13% of the scrolls, most overwritten by collisions. Per retained scroll the new load is
cheaper, and 233 MB is simply the true weight of holding all 320,958 scrolls plus the
38 MB source (the C# harness process measured 272 MB holding the same content). The
work is not the same work, so this is not a regression — but two ultra-scale costs are
worth naming: **jump + first view at the maximum coordinate: 57.7 ms** (first outline
build for the giant book; inner view at home: 4.7 ms), and **`set_scroll` ≈ 9.4 ms per
call on ultra**, dominated by the perf pass's O(scrolls) `serialized_len` arithmetic
walk. At agi scale that walk is the 0.09 ms/call the perf pass measured; on ultra it is
a real typing-latency number, flagged below rather than hidden.

## Residuals / Will's calls

- **Publish-day pin swap:** when libphext 0.5.0 reaches crates.io (Will's act under
  the freeze), replace the git pin with `version = "0.5.0"` — one line in
  `native/core/Cargo.toml` + lock refresh.
- **No seal/binary UI in the FLTK app** (core layer only, by design). Adding
  open-sealed / save-sealed or a paste-binary affordance is a product decision.
- **`load_path` unchanged:** it still rejects non-UTF-8 files with the existing
  message. `Document::from_bytes` (the ingest path) exists in the core; routing the
  app's Open through it would change open semantics, so it waits for Will's word.
- **`set_scroll` byte-count walk at ultra scale** (~9.4 ms/keystroke, above):
  pre-existing perf-pass design, fine at agi scale; worth an incremental-totals
  rework only if ultra editing latency starts to matter.
- **WinForms parity surface, if ever needed:** libphext-cs 0.5.0's `BinaryScroll` /
  `Phext.Seal` (already on cs exo, awaiting Will's NuGet publish) — the WinForms app
  vendors its own phext sources today and does not consume the library.

