## Addendum — publish-day pin swap (2026-11-08, task 58.10.5/11.11.15/11.10.18)

Will published libphext-rs v0.5.0 to crates.io on 2026-11-08, closing the
"publish-day pin swap" residual above. Commit **`75789a9141dafe0193fe1e377f7a0660e2acc6d2`**
on `emise/ultra-open-fix` (parent `636989e`, fast-forward via aletheia-core SSH as wbic16,
force disabled, remote ref re-verified via the GitHub API at `75789a9`). All work ran on
aletheia-core (cargo 1.94.0) in a fresh clone of the branch. No libphext repo was touched.

### Registry verification (before touching the tree)

A scratch probe crate depending on `libphext = { version = "0.5.0", features = ["binary", "xz"] }`
resolved from the registry and compiled a program naming every libphext item the notepad
core consumes — `libphext::binary` (11 items incl. `BINARY_SCROLL_HEADER`), `libphext::seal`
(`seal`, `seal_bytes`, `unseal`, `unseal_string`, `SEAL_PRESET` = 6), and the `libphext::phext`
surface (Coordinate, `default_coordinate`, `COORDINATE_MINIMUM`, break constants,
`explode`/`implode` round trip). Cargo resolution itself proves the `binary` and `xz`
features exist on the published crate (an unknown feature fails resolution).

- Resolved: **libphext 0.5.0**, source `registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index`
- Cargo.lock checksum: **`c8f8df75a786bc56bc7261e068e26cfa089341a26feb7d0c2a299ef2a6b4da8c`**
  (identical in the probe's lockfile and the notepad's regenerated lockfile)

### The diff (2 files, +7/−6)

- `native/core/Cargo.toml` — the git pin
  (`git = "https://github.com/wbic16/libphext-rs", rev = "e85d8b5…"`) replaced with
  `libphext = { version = "0.5.0", features = ["binary", "xz"] }`; the pin-rationale
  comment updated to record the publish.
- `native/Cargo.lock` — regenerated via `cargo update -p libphext`: libphext's source
  line moves from the git rev to the registry with the checksum above; no other
  package changed version.

### API drift

**None.** The core compiled and passed unchanged against the published crate — the
published 0.5.0 surface is the surface the git rev carried, at every point the core
consumes it. (One false alarm during verification was the probe's own bug: it passed
`implode` a reference where the crate — both revisions — takes the map by value; the
core's call already matched.)

### Re-verification against the published crate

- **Core suite: 37 passed, 0 failed** (`cargo test --release -p phext-notepad-core`) —
  the same 37 as the git pin (23 baseline + 8 binary + 2 seal + 4 document).
- **FLTK app compiles:** `cargo check --release -p phext-notepad` finished clean.
- **Ultra full fidelity** (`agi_bench`, source SHA-256
  `fd68c5b0fd93a7388516fc20ae248f7fb16ebe2b1bb9b9b78d2ceedf8da8ac93`):
  **320,958 scrolls** loaded; load matches `libphext::explode`: true; serialize matches
  `libphext::implode`: true; **`serialize == input bytes`: true** (37,953,029 bytes);
  fixed point: true; jump to `9.9.9/9.9.9/9.9.223854` works. Load 151.2 ms median,
  peak RSS after load 238,472 KiB — same figures class as the git pin (166.2 ms /
  238,396 KiB).
- **Parity harness spot checks** (the parity run's own harness, `core-link` repointed
  at the swapped tree): with the original fixture set, the published-crate core's full
  output — constructed doc, ingest output, per-scroll manifest, 41-case decode-verdict
  battery, 1 MiB + 13 payload, and **all five seals (agi, cyoa, doc, empty, small)** —
  is **byte-identical to the stored rs-crate reference outputs** (`out-ref` from the
  parity run). Cross-unseal both directions: refbin unseals the new core's agi seal to
  the exact fixture bytes; the new core unseals the reference's agi and cyoa seals to
  the exact originals.
  - One discrepancy chased down en route: a first harness run against `~/fixtures`
    showed `agi.xz` differing from the stored reference — in *both* a fresh reference
    build and the new core. Cause: that directory holds a different agi.phext snapshot
    (SHA-256 `6eaead55…`) than the parity run's fixture set (SHA-256 `5871b8f4…`, still
    on disk in `~/phext-ultra/corpus`). Against the parity run's own fixtures every
    byte matches; the seal was never the variable. Fresh ref vs fresh core seals are
    byte-identical on either fixture set.

### Locations

